```latex
\begin{proposition}
Let $r$ and $c$ be positive integers, and let $a_1,\dots,a_r$ and $b_1,\dots,b_c$ be arbitrary elements of $\{0,1\}$. Consider $r\times c$ arrays $A=(x_{ij})$ with every entry $x_{ij}\in\{0,1\}$. Row $i$ has parity $a_i$ when its entry sum is congruent to $a_i$ modulo $2$. Column $j$ has parity $b_j$ under the same definition. Call $A$ \emph{valid} if
\[
\sum_{j=1}^{c} x_{ij}\equiv a_i \pmod 2 \quad\text{for } 1\le i\le r
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\sum_{i=1}^{r} x_{ij}\equiv b_j \pmod 2 \quad\text{for } 1\le j\le c.
\]
We count labeled arrays. Two arrays are equal only if they agree in every entry, so permuting rows or columns generally gives a different array. The number $N$ of valid arrays is
\[
N=
\begin{cases}
0, & \text{if } \displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^r a_i\not\equiv\sum_{j=1}^c b_j \pmod 2,\\[2ex]
2^{(r-1)(c-1)}, & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Throughout the proof, $\equiv$ denotes congruence modulo $2$, and an empty sum equals $0$. Each residue class modulo $2$ contains exactly one element of $\{0,1\}$. A congruence $x\equiv y$ with $x\in\{0,1\}$ therefore determines $x$ from $y$.

We first show that the parity totals must agree. Suppose $A$ is valid. Adding all row sums and adding all column sums count the same entries, so
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i=1}^r a_i
&\equiv \sum_{i=1}^r\sum_{j=1}^c x_{ij} && \text{(row conditions)}\\
&= \sum_{j=1}^c\sum_{i=1}^r x_{ij} && \text{(both sums count every entry once)}\\
&\equiv \sum_{j=1}^c b_j && \text{(column conditions).}
\end{align*}
Hence no valid array exists when the prescribed totals disagree, and $N=0$.

Now suppose the totals agree:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:totals}
\sum_{i=1}^r a_i\equiv\sum_{j=1}^c b_j.
\end{equation}
Let $\mathcal B$ be the set of $(r-1)\times(c-1)$ arrays with entries in $\{0,1\}$, indexed by $1\le i\le r-1$ and $1\le j\le c-1$. Each of the $(r-1)(c-1)$ entries can be chosen freely, so $|\mathcal B|=2^{(r-1)(c-1)}$. Given $B=(x_{ij})\in\mathcal B$, we place $B$ in the upper-left corner of an $r\times c$ array and define the remaining entries in $\{0,1\}$ by
\begin{align}
x_{ic}&\equiv a_i-\sum_{j=1}^{c-1}x_{ij}, && 1\le i\le r-1, \label{eq:last-column}\\
x_{rj}&\equiv b_j-\sum_{i=1}^{r-1}x_{ij}, && 1\le j\le c-1, \label{eq:last-row}\\
x_{rc}&\equiv a_r-\sum_{j=1}^{c-1}x_{rj}. \label{eq:corner}
\end{align}
The entries defined by \eqref{eq:last-column} lie in the last column above the corner, and those defined by \eqref{eq:last-row} lie in the last row to the left of the corner. These two parts of the border are disjoint, and neither contains the corner $(r,c)$. The right-hand sides of \eqref{eq:last-column} and \eqref{eq:last-row} use only entries of $B$, and the right-hand side of \eqref{eq:corner} uses only entries defined by \eqref{eq:last-row}. Each remaining entry is therefore defined exactly once, and we denote the resulting array by $\Phi(B)$.

Next we check that $\Phi(B)$ is valid. Rows $1,\dots,r-1$ have their prescribed parities by \eqref{eq:last-column}, row $r$ by \eqref{eq:corner}, and columns $1,\dots,c-1$ by \eqref{eq:last-row}. Only column $c$ remains. All of its entries are already fixed, including the corner, which was chosen to give row $r$ parity $a_r$. The parity of column $c$ must therefore be checked, since it is not a free choice. Let
\[
S=\sum_{i=1}^r\sum_{j=1}^c x_{ij}
\]
be the sum of all entries of $\Phi(B)$. Removing the first $c-1$ columns from $S$ leaves the last column, so
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i=1}^r x_{ic}
&= S-\sum_{j=1}^{c-1}\sum_{i=1}^r x_{ij}\\
&\equiv \sum_{i=1}^r a_i-\sum_{j=1}^{c-1}b_j\\
&\equiv \sum_{j=1}^c b_j-\sum_{j=1}^{c-1}b_j\\
&= b_c.
\end{align*}
The second line holds because every row has its prescribed parity, which gives $S\equiv\sum_{i=1}^r a_i$, and because column $j$ has parity $b_j$ for each $j<c$. The third line uses \eqref{eq:totals}. Thus $\Phi(B)$ is valid.

Finally, we show that every valid array arises from exactly one block. For an $r\times c$ array $A$, let $R(A)$ denote its upper-left $(r-1)\times(c-1)$ block. The construction of $\Phi(B)$ leaves the block unchanged, so $R(\Phi(B))=B$ for every $B\in\mathcal B$. Conversely, let $A$ be valid. Rearranged, its row condition for each $i<r$ is \eqref{eq:last-column}, its column condition for each $j<c$ is \eqref{eq:last-row}, and its row condition for $i=r$ is \eqref{eq:corner}. Taken in that order, these congruences determine every border entry of $A$ from its block, so $A=\Phi(R(A))$. Hence $\Phi$ is a bijection from $\mathcal B$ onto the set of valid arrays, with inverse $R$, and
\[
N=|\mathcal B|=2^{(r-1)(c-1)}.
\]

If $r=1$ or $c=1$, then $(r-1)(c-1)=0$. In that case $\mathcal B$ contains only the empty array, so $|\mathcal B|=1=2^0$. The argument above applies without change, with empty sums equal to $0$ and with \eqref{eq:last-column} or \eqref{eq:last-row} vacuous. For example, when $r=c=1$ only \eqref{eq:corner} remains, and it gives $x_{11}=a_1$. In that case \eqref{eq:totals} reads $a_1\equiv b_1$, so the single entry also satisfies the column condition.
\end{proof}
